item.description:
Project Officer/Editor (State of the Industry Report)
ChargeUp Europe
Brussels, Belgium
Contract:
Fixed-term, 6 months
Expected start date:
15 October 2026
Application deadline:
23 August 2026 (COB)
About ChargeUp Europe
ChargeUp Europe is the voice of the electric vehicle (EV) charging infrastructure industry. Our growing membership is committed to delivering a seamless charging experience for EV drivers, incentivising investment and creating a consumer-centric, open market model for charging infrastructure in Europe. ChargeUp Europe’s mission is to push for an infrastructure roll-out that starts from the consumer needs, makes EV driving convenient across borders and so strives for an open-market model supported by open standards and protocols, removing market barriers related to concessions, grids, data sharing or building codes.
The EV charging infrastructure industry is at the forefront of the twin green and digital transitions. It is an innovative, fast-evolving, dynamic sector. Investments into charging Infrastructure will grow from €5 billion in 2021 to over €15 billion in 2030, with an expected growth of the workforce by +270 % by 2030.
Function
ChargeUp Europe is looking for a driven, organised professional to join its dynamic secretariat as Project Officer for ChargeUp Europe's flagship "State of the Industry" report.
The role in a nutshell:
The Project Officer will manage the production of the fourth (2027) edition of ChargeUp Europe's flagship "State of the Industry" report.
Main responsibilities:
Lead the full production process and delivery of the 2027 State of the Industry report from planning through publication;
Monitor the project timeline and ensure milestones are met to meet delivery on time;
Work with the report’s external research partner (EY Parthenon), and ChargeUp Europe members, to analyse data and edit the report;
Coordinate and edit input from the members (e.g. case studies), including with ChargeUp Europe’s Data Review Task Force and Communications Committee;
Organise meetings and review sessions as part of the editing and validation process;
Coordinate with the graphic designer on layout and publication;
Proofread, fact-check and ensure consistency of the report.
Candidate profile
Our ideal candidate would combine:
1–3 years of project management or research experience;
Strong project management skills;
Excellent organisational ability;
High attention to detail;
Native or near-native English;
Experience in e-mobility or a related sector (preferred but not required);
Ability to work independently and collaboratively;
Familiarity with project management tools (an advantage).
Drive and commitment are more important than academic achievements or experience. If you do not check all the boxes above but are a go-getter committed to delivering excellent service to the members, you are welcome to apply and tell us about that in your cover letter.
Offer
ChargeUp Europe offers:
A fixed-term (6 months) full-time contract, under Belgian law;
A competitive salary;
Lunch vouchers;
A pension plan;
A medical plan (DKV);
A mobile device, mobile phone subscription, and laptop;
25 days of holidays;
A flexible teleworking regime;
A teleworking allowance;
A representation allowance;
A monthly public transport subscription;
Direct exposure to experienced, committed members;
A location in the heart of Brussels’ EU district, easily accessible by public transportation.
Hiring process
Interested candidates should send their application in English by
23 August 2026
COB at the latest to
secretariat@chargeupeurope.eu
, mentioning “Project Officer” in the subject line. The application should include:
a CV;
a motivation letter (not written by AI);
a short written sample (e.g. letter, social media post, blog, article, position paper, academic assignment etc.).
Please note that incomplete applications will not be taken into account. Applications will be examined on a rolling basis, with interviews starting already during the application period; applicants are therefore strongly encouraged to apply early, as the recruitment process will stop once a suitable candidate has been found.
The location of the position is Brussels, and the contract is offered under Belgian law. ChargeUp Europe will require candidates to demonstrate that they have the right to live and work in the EU. ChargeUp Europe is unable to provide relocation assistance or visa support.
ChargeUp Europe values diversity and encourages applications from all suitably qualified candidates regardless of age, gender, race, disability, sexual orientation, religion or ethnic background.
Only short-listed candidates will be contacted for interviews.
Don't forget to mention EuroBrussels when applying.

Political Adviser - Food Policy
Eurogroup for Animals
Brussels, Belgium
From € 3,811 up to € 4,611 per month
Drive and coordinate sustainable food policy advocacy across the EU, bridging the gap between high-level legislative lobbying and national-level action to empower member organizations and accelerate systemic food systems transformation.
Eurogroup for Animals is the pan-European animal protection organisation which unites the voices of more than 100 member organisations. We work to improve the welfare of as many animals as possible by leading and delivering coordinated advocacy and campaigns at European level. Eurogroup for Animals uses its knowledge and expertise to challenge EU decision makers and bring about positive change. By uniting our member organisations and working together we magnify national activities to get our message across at European level.
Eurogroup for Animals’ farm animal programme aims at reducing suffering of animals on farms by advocating for transformational change of our farming systems, shifting toward sustainable food production and consumption with higher animal welfare and more plant-based food.
Purpose of the role
As Political Adviser - Food Policy, you will drive advocacy and political engagement at EU level to advance sustainable public food procurement and food systems transformation. You will develop and implement lobbying strategies to influence key EU legislative and policy files, particularly the revision of the Public Procurement Directive, as well as relevant elements of the Common Agricultural Policy and Common Market Organisation Regulation, while empowering Eurogroup for Animals’ Member Organisations to leverage policy opportunities at national level. The successful candidate will play a key role in translating policy developments into public affairs and advocacy strategies, while supporting practical actions that accelerate food systems transformation towards higher animal welfare and more plant-based food consumption and production.
Primary responsibilities
Lead EU-level advocacy and political engagement on the revision and implementation of the Public Procurement Directive;
Develop and implement targeted lobbying strategies to influence key EU policymakers and stakeholders on public food procurement, Common Agricultural Policy and Common Market Organisation files;
Monitor and analyse legislative and policy developments, translating them into advocacy opportunities and providing timely strategic intelligence to Member Organisations;
Empower and coordinate Member Organisations to engage strategically in EU and national-level advocacy and lobbying on public food procurement and food systems transformation;
Build and maintain strategic relationships with EU institutions, Member State representatives, coalition partners, civil society organisations and relevant food-system actors, while representing Eurogroup for Animals in meetings and external events;
Develop evidence-based advocacy messages and policy positions in collaboration with internal experts and Member Organisations;
In close collaboration with Member Organisations, support the replication and scaling of successful local public food procurement initiatives;
In collaboration with the Food Policy Lead and communications team, develop advocacy materials and toolkits to support Member Organisations' engagement on public food procurement, CAP national plans and other relevant food policy files;
In collaboration with the Food Policy Lead, develop advocacy materials for extending the animal welfare labelling in the Common Market Organisation Regulation;
In collaboration with the Food Policy Lead, support the communications team in the development of campaigns on public food procurement and the Common Agricultural Policy.
Person specification
Essential skills & qualifications
Relevant academic background and/or demonstrated expertise in sustainable food systems, food policy, public procurement or related fields;
Proven experience in EU-level lobbying, public affairs and policy-making, particularly within EU institutions;
Strong understanding of EU policymaking processes and institutions;
Confidence in engaging with policymakers on complex legislative files;
Ability to translate technical policy analysis into persuasive advocacy messages;
Experience developing and implementing advocacy or public affairs strategies;
Excellent stakeholder engagement and relationship-building skills;
Strong coordination skills and ability to work collaboratively in a cross-functional team;
Well-organised and focused on concrete results and project deliverables;
Fluency in English.
Desirable skills and qualifications
Experience managing complex legislative and policy files;
Strong understanding of national-level policy processes in one or more Member States;
Experience working with membership-based organisations or networks;
Expertise in agriculture, aquaculture, animal welfare or related policy areas;
Experience working on public procurement policy;
Additional EU languages.
The person should have affinity with animal welfare and relate to the
vision and mission of Eurogroup for Animals
.
The values that we at Eurogroup for Animals adhere to are:
collaboration; entrepreneurship; impact and a focus on member organisations
.
Practicalities
The position is a full-time permanent employee contract (we have today the funds for 2 years). The position is based in Brussels, or requires the successful candidate to be present in Brussels approximately 50% of the time.
Salary ranges from €3,811/month to €4,611/month depending on experience.
How to apply?
Please send your application letter, your salary expectations and CV, in English, to
jobs@eurogroupforanimals.org
.
Application deadline:
4 September 2026
Don't forget to mention EuroBrussels when applying.

Adviseur Europese Zaken (Public Affairs/EU Policy)
Federation of the Dutch Pension Funds
Brussels, Belgium
Van Europese regelgeving naar impact voor de Nederlandse pensioensector.
Wil jij impact maken op Europese wet- en regelgeving die miljoenen Nederlanders raakt? Als Adviseur Europese Zaken ben je de schakel tussen Brussel en de Nederlandse pensioensector. Je volgt Europese ontwikkelingen op de voet, analyseert de impact voor pensioenfondsen en vertaalt deze naar heldere standpunten en effectieve public affairs- en lobbystrategieën.
Dit zijn wij
De Pensioenfederatie is de belangenbehartiger van vrijwel alle Nederlandse pensioenfondsen en speelt een sleutelrol in zowel Den Haag als Brussel. Samen met onze 140 leden werken we aan een toekomstbestendig en kwalitatief hoogwaardig pensioenstelsel. Vanuit ons kantoor in Brussel, in het hart van de Europese wijk, opereren we midden in het internationale speelveld van de EU instellingen.
Voor ons team in Brussel zoeken we een analytische en politiek-bestuurlijk sensitieve adviseur die impact wil maken in een internationale omgeving. In deze rol werk je op het snijvlak van strategie en uitvoering: je vertaalt Europese beleidsontwikkelingen naar concrete acties en helpt de belangen van de Nederlandse pensioensector effectief te positioneren binnen Europese instellingen en netwerken.
Dit ben jij
Je bent een analytische en politiek-bestuurlijk sensitieve adviseur die energie krijgt van Europese vraagstukken. Je volgt ontwikkelingen in Brussel op de voet en weet snel tot de kern te komen: wat betekent dit en wat moeten we ermee?
Je schakelt moeiteloos tussen strategie en uitvoering. Of het nu gaat om het analyseren van EU-wetgeving of het voorbereiden van een lobbyinzet: jij weet wat nodig is en zet dat om in concrete stappen. Je stelt prioriteiten, houdt overzicht en zorgt dat zaken ook echt gebeuren.
Je werkt graag samen en beweegt je gemakkelijk in een netwerk van uiteenlopende stakeholders, zowel nationaal als internationaal. In die internationale context vertegenwoordig je de Pensioenfederatie met overtuiging en gevoel voor verhoudingen.
Met jouw proactieve houding, scherpe analyse en gevoel voor samenwerking maak je zichtbaar impact.
Wat ga je doen?
Je werkt aan uiteenlopende Europese dossiers op het gebied van public affairs, beleidsontwikkeling en belangenbehartiging. Je:
monitort, analyseert en beïnvloedt Europese wet- en regelgeving die relevant is voor de Nederlandse pensioensector;
ontwikkelt en voert de Europese public affairs-strategie en lobbyagenda uit;
signaleert politieke en maatschappelijke ontwikkelingen en vertaalt deze naar beleidsadviezen en standpunten;
vertegenwoordigt de Pensioenfederatie in Europese koepelorganisaties, werkgroepen en internationale bijeenkomsten;
onderhoudt en versterkt een relevant Europees netwerk van beleidsmakers, toezichthouders en stakeholders;
bereidt briefings, werkbezoeken en internationale overleggen voor bestuur en directie voor.
Wat breng je mee
Je bent een stevige gesprekspartner die complexe vraagstukken snel doorgrondt en verschillende belangen weet te verbinden. Je schakelt gemakkelijk tussen strategie en uitvoering en voelt je thuis in een politiek-bestuurlijke omgeving.
Daarnaast heb je:
een afgeronde wo-opleiding, bijvoorbeeld in rechten, politicologie, bestuurskunde, economie of Europese studies;
2 tot 6 jaar relevante werkervaring, bij voorkeur binnen een belangenorganisatie, overheid, Europese instelling of complexe maatschappelijke organisatie;
uitstekende mondelinge en schriftelijke communicatievaardigheden, zowel in het Engels als in het Nederlands;
ervaring met beleidsontwikkeling en het beïnvloeden van beleidsprocessen;
een goed ontwikkelde politieke antenne en gevoel voor bestuurlijke verhoudingen;
ervaring met stakeholdermanagement en samenwerking in complexe omgevingen;
affiniteit met pensioenen, wet- en regelgeving of financiële vraagstukken (pré).
Voor deze functie woon je bij voorkeur in (de omgeving van) Brussel. Woon je momenteel elders, dan ben je bereid om naar de Brusselse regio te verhuizen.
Waarom dit bij jou past
Je wilt impact maken op Europese dossiers die er echt toe doen. De combinatie van inhoud, strategie en internationale samenwerking geeft jou energie. Je voelt je prettig in een omgeving waarin belangen samenkomen en waar je actief bijdraagt aan het positioneren van de pensioensector in Europa.
De dynamiek van Brussel en het werken aan meerdere dossiers tegelijk passen bij jouw manier van werken: scherp, proactief en gericht op resultaat.
Wat bieden wij jou?
Wij bieden een uitdagende baan in het hart van een grote en dynamische sector, met de voordelen van een kleine en wendbare organisatie. Je krijgt veel ruimte, verantwoordelijkheid en de kans om zichtbaar impact te maken.
aantrekkelijk salaris, afhankelijk van kennis en ervaring;
riante thuiswerkvergoeding en budget voor je thuiswerkplek;
ruime mogelijkheden tot hybride werken;
uitstekende pensioenregeling;
volledige OV-reiskostenvergoeding (1e klas);
een sleutelrol in een sector die volop in beweging is;
een informele, hechte werksfeer binnen een klein en professioneel team;
een werkplek in Brussel, nabij het Europees Parlement en de Europese Commissie, met uitzicht én impact.
Interesse?
Stuur je motivatie en cv-uiterlijk
19 augustus 2026
.
Sollicitatieproces
De gesprekken vinden plaats in twee rondes. We vragen kandidaten om alvast rekening te houden met de volgende data:
eerste gespreksronde: donderdag 27 augustus;
tweede gespreksronde: vrijdagmiddag 4 september.
Heb je inhoudelijke vragen? Neem gerust contact op met Matthies Verstegen via +32 476 870 847.
Apply Now
Don't forget to mention EuroBrussels when applying.

Communications Assistant
EuroCommerce
Brussels, Belgium
Employment type:
Full-time
Start date:
As soon as possible
Want to kick-start your career at the crossroads of communications, digital content, and European affairs?
EuroCommerce, the voice of retail and wholesale in Europe, is looking for a creative, digitally savvy, and motivated Communications Assistant to join our team in Brussels.
If you're passionate about storytelling, social media, design, websites, AI tools, and making an impact across Europe, this could be your next big opportunity.
What you will be doing
As part of our Communications team, you'll help shape how EuroCommerce communicates with members, policymakers, stakeholders, and the wider public.
Key Responsibilities
Create engaging content
You'll bring ideas to life across multiple channels by:
Designing visually appealing visuals and presentations;
Creating and editing digital content, including graphics, videos, and animations;
Supporting campaigns that help tell the story of Europe's retail and wholesale sector;
Experimenting with new communication formats and digital trends.
Help drive our digital presence
You'll play a key role in strengthening our online visibility by:
Managing and updating website content;
Supporting newsletter creation and email campaigns;
Exploring AI-powered tools to improve content creation, analytics, and workflows;
Contributing ideas to enhance user engagement and digital performance.
Be a brand champion
You’ll help ensure everything we publish looks and feels consistently "EuroCommerce" by:
Maintaining strong visual and editorial standards;
Supporting the creation of templates, presentations, and communication materials;
Helping colleagues apply branding and communication best practices.
Connect with our community
You’ll support communication with our members and stakeholders by:
Maintaining contact databases and mailing lists;
Supporting member communication platforms and user requests;
Helping deliver targeted and relevant communications;
Ensuring data quality and GDPR compliance.
Who we are looking for
We are looking for potential. You might be a great fit if you
Have a Bachelor's degree in Communications, Marketing, Political Sciences, European Studies, or a related field;
Are fluent (or near-native) in English; additional European languages are a bonus;
Love creating content and have an eye for design and digital trends;
Are comfortable with Microsoft Office and eager to learn new digital tools;
Have experience, or a strong interest, in social media, community management, or stakeholder engagement;
Are curious about how decisions are made in the EU and want to learn more;
Take initiative, enjoy solving problems, and aren't afraid to bring fresh ideas to the table;
Thrive in a fast-moving environment where no two days are the same.
What’s in it for you
A full-time role on a permanent contract;
An international and collaborative workplace in the heart of Brussels;
Opportunities to build skills in communications, digital media, design, and EU policy;
Hands-on experience with high-impact projects and campaigns;
Exposure to European decision-making and industry leaders;
A chance to make your mark and grow your professional network.
Why join EuroCommerce?
At EuroCommerce, your ideas matter. You'll gain real responsibility from day one, work with colleagues from across Europe, and help shape conversations that impact millions of businesses and consumers.
If you're curious, creative, digitally minded, and ready to grow, we'd love to hear from you.
How to Apply
Please send your CV and a short motivation letter to
bastings@eurocommerce.eu
with the subject line: Application - Communications Assistant. Applications will be reviewed on a rolling basis.
Don't forget to mention EuroBrussels when applying.

Grants Administration - Intern
Finance Watch
Brussels, Belgium
€ 1.056,00 per month
Finance Watch is a Brussels-based NGO that works to make finance serve society and the planet. We are looking for a Grants Intern to strengthen our Operations & Projects department.
This is an exciting position to support a growing team, starting as soon as possible.
This role would suit a person with strong pitching skills, who is interested in developing their grant application skillset and is highly motivated by our mission. The
Grants Intern
will be part of the Operations & Projects department of Finance Watch, comprising Finance, HR & Operations.
The
Grants Intern
will report to the Head of Communications & Operations and work closely with the whole team on a daily basis.
The ideal candidate would have native proficiency in English, grant management relevant studies, and have an outstanding capacity to report on our activities to funders. A willingness to learn about financial regulation is indispensable, and French or German language skills would be a plus.
Ensuring a high-quality experience for all funders
The core focus of the internship is to ensure an optimal experience for all existing funders. The Grants Administration Intern is the contact point for funding contracts. They will coordinate input from the team in funding applications and reporting.
Grant making
Draft responses to due diligence questions from funders, in collaboration with the Operations Department;
Ensure compliance with the association’s rules, as described in the Articles of Association;
Comply with the internal rules, and make sure the management team is involved early in the process of new funding opportunities;
Draft a final narrative review of funding proposals, and share it with the supervisor for sign-off.
Collaboration and buy-in
Involve staff members in the crafting of the funding narrative (Management team decides who will be best placed to contribute);
Work closely with partners in case of consortium funding (e.g. LIFE);
Inform the team when a funding opportunity is about to be signed, and when it is signed, outlining the key elements and deliverables.
Report on activities
Coordinate the reporting to donors;
Inform colleagues about upcoming reportings on their activities;
Make sure the deadlines are respected, taking into account the time for internal review by all staff members;
Give assistance to colleagues involved in the making of reportings to donors;
Ensure project tracking tools and information systems are always up-to-date;
Maintain a birds-eye view of overall reporting requirements, and grant timelines for projects;
Collaborate with the Head of Operations to deliver financial reporting;
Work closely with the Communications department to create materials that meet the needs, objectives and specifications of funding reportings;
Draft reports on funding activities to the Board;
Draft a final narrative review of reporting to funders, and propose it for sign-off to the supervisor.
Follow-up
Follow-up all grants from submission to contract signing and by liaising with all relevant entities during the process;
Monitor & evaluate progress;
Measuring the impact of grants/activities;
Provide advice on grant application processes and propose improvements;
Develop a process for a more efficient and standardised grant review process to ensure a timely and smooth flow of activities across all stakeholders;
Complete a table of KPIs amongst all grants;
Propose improvements to the funder’s journey;
Develop, maintain and provide guidance or standard templates for all grant related activities:
Standardised slides;
One-page report on activities.
Communication to Funders & ex-funders
Contribute to the definition of the listing of funders and ex-funders;
Produce in coordination with the Communications Department, a bimonthly newsletter to funders with key messages (leveraging content from other newsletters);
Thank donors for their support.
Profile/Qualifications
Essential
You are motivated by and committed to Finance Watch’s mission;
You are good at pitching stories and selling ideas;
Outstanding editing and writing skills in English are essential (native proficiency);
You have experience or training in grant management;
Ability to manage a substantial and varied workload effectively, including managing competing demands, maintaining accuracy, and coordinating activities across diverse departments within the organisation;
Excellent project management skills.
Desirable
Bachelor degree or equivalent;
Knowledge of the European grants landscape, especially for NGOs;
Ability to work with a CRM tool (database & mailing system);
Good digital skills, and familiarity with Excel;
Good judgement, to assess the most effective means of communicating information for the purpose of achieving the grant’s goals;
Strong team player, the ability to listen and collaborate with a wide range of colleagues;
Excellent interpersonal skills, and experience of working in a multinational/-cultural environment;
Results-oriented and self-motivated.
Offer
A 3 to 6 months paid internship;
A multidisciplinary and multicultural working environment;
A laptop (for professional use);
Holidays: 2 days per month;
High exposure to a large and diverse set of stakeholders;
Partial home working and part-time are negotiable;
The position is based in Brussels.
Equal employment opportunity
Finance Watch is committed to the principle of equal employment opportunity for all employees, regardless of sex, marital status, nationality, religion, age, sexual orientation and any other characteristics unrelated to the performance of the job.
Know that we are particularly eager to increase the representation of women, “non-Caucasian” and disabled people within the Finance Watch staff.
Selection will be in accordance with objective, job related criteria and the appointment will be on the basis of applicants’ merits and abilities.
Internship allowance
The intern will benefit from an allowance and a pay slip.
Occupational allowance: 1.056,00€ per month, prorata temporis;
Lunch allowance: 6.91 € per day of work;
Transportation allowance: 34 € min per month, if living in Belgium.
Application guidelines
Your application should include the following documents
A Curriculum Vitae;
A short motivation letter with:
A mention that you
hold a work visa valid for the entire duration of the internship (3 to 6 months
);
A mention that you meet the following requirements:
You are a
job seeker in Belgium
(CIP); or
You are a
student from a European university
with or without a University agreement – proof of registration is required; or
You are a candidate from another European country in the context of an
international training programme
(Erasmus+, Eurodyssey, etc.).
Any application that does not include confirmation of your eligibility will be disregarded.
Send your application by email to:
recruitment@finance-watch.org
. Please quote “Grant Administration – Intern” in the title of your e-mail.
Interviews will take place online and/or in Brussels.
Only shortlisted candidates will be contacted.
The position remains open until filled.
Don't forget to mention EuroBrussels when applying.

(Senior) Research and Advocacy Officer - Public Finance
Finance Watch
Brussels, Belgium
Fixed-term contract until 30 April 2027
Finance Watch, the Brussels-based public-interest advocacy association dedicated to making finance serve society, is looking for a (Senior) Research and Advocacy Officer to strengthen its team by undertaking research and advocacy work, as well as capacity-building efforts.
The (Senior) Research and Advocacy Officer will join the Research & Advocacy Department, and will report to the Head of Research & Advocacy. Each member of this department works on specific areas of financial regulation and delivers:
policy-oriented research from a public-interest perspective, including the identification of the main issues and related legislative solutions;
advocacy, including building policymakers’ support for Finance Watch’s proposals;
regular contribution to communications, convening and capacity-building efforts in relevant areas of expertise.
The Research & Advocacy Department has regular direct contacts with international, EU and national policymakers. Additionally, this department works in close collaboration with the entire Finance Watch organisation, including its members, and with other civil society partners.
Job description
The (Senior) Research and Advocacy Officer takes responsibility for legislative files and initiatives related to their subject matter expertise and defined area of responsibility. Specifically, these include public finance architecture (EU fiscal rules, debt sustainability analysis, national reform plans) and consideration of sustainability/climate risks within the macroeconomic and debt sustainability projections.
Specific topics of responsibility are defined in the job description and redefined annually, based on the work program and our multi-year strategies.
Core Responsibilities
Policy and advocacy
Conduct policy-oriented research and make policy proposals;
Draft legislative amendments;
Develop
policy positions
in collaboration with the Head of Research and Advocacy;
Represent
Finance Watch externally with policymakers, regulators and supervisors, financial industry participants and civil society (bilateral meetings, conferences and other events);
Participate in industry events, as well as high-level events with policymakers, in order to represent civil society’s views;
Develop advocacy tactics and plans;
Execute advocacy plans targeted at policymakers and regulators;
Build and maintain relationships with policymakers and supervisors on an ongoing basis.
Work with Finance Watch members
Interact with Finance Watch
members
on the files they are responsible for, via:
Posting on the Members’ Hub;
Organising regular working groups (at least every two months);
Consulting members on the draft publications ahead of the release.
Contribute to the efforts of identifying and engaging
new members
;
Interact and, where appropriate, coordinate advocacy efforts with other civil society organisations (CSO), academics and experts on the topics related to their areas of expertise.
Contribution to awareness-raising
Contribute to the debate and comment on
social media
;
Communicate regularly with journalists on the files they are responsible for;
Draft blogs and
articles
, when appropriate;
Contribute to the Finance Watch
newsletters
;
Organise
events
(workshops, roundtable discussions, panel discussions, conferences etc.) to support research and advocacy efforts;
Contribute to design communications and campaigning strategies.
Data management
Contribute to the growth and maintenance of the Finance Watch
database
of relevant contacts (policymakers, Civil society organisations, academics etc.);
Contribute to drafting funding applications and the
periodic reporting
to funders/donors.
What does success in this job look like?
Finance Watch strengthens its profile as a
leading
public-interest, expert
voice
in the respective subject matter area;
Finance Watch develops a comprehensive, communicable, consistent and
high-quality corpus
of policy analysis and recommendations;
Policymakers (regulators) and supervisors take onboard Finance Watch policy proposals and recommendations as part of the supervisory and legislative process;
Finance Watch is able to
anticipate major moments for action
, with a mix of reactive and proactive outputs;
Finance Watch delivers its Research & Advocacy strategy and objectives
on time
and properly
reports
on the actions and results (for the respective area of responsibility);
Collaboration
and communication with the rest of the organisation is effective and transparent, and contributes to a strong team spirit.
Desired Profile/Qualifications
At least 5 years of experience in the domain of public finance at the EU or Member State level, including in the application of the EU fiscal rules (multiannual financial framework, national reform plans etc.), macroeconomic research/analysis, debt sustainability analysis;
Experience with/knowledge of economic analysis of climate change (modelling, scenario analysis, cost estimates etc.) is an advantage;
Strong experience in conducting policy analysis and writing reports/briefs;
Understanding of the main issues at stake with the EU public finance architecture, economic governance, debt and deficit rules, relevant EU and national mechanisms/institutions;
Knowledge of the climate risk agenda;
Understanding of the functioning of public finance in general and ability to analyse a diverse range of financial issues;
Strong understanding of the EU legislative process, financial regulation and supervision;
Master’s Degree or equivalent level in a relevant field of studies (macroeconomics/economics, political science, law etc.
Professional Interests
Commitment to Finance Watch’s mission of “making finance serve society”;
Interest in and ability to understand other public finance topics such as state aid, procurement, monetary policy, as well as private finance (banking, insurance, financial markets, retail/consumer finance);
Interest in financial reforms in Europe, relevant international mechanisms and institutions.
Skills
Ability to work and communicate with multiple stakeholders, internally and externally;
Experience in representing an organisation externally and public speaking, including at international events;
Effective workload management, including managing competing demands and maintaining high levels of accuracy and reliability;
Ability to analyse finance topics and regulation thereof, identify issues and propose solutions;
Ability to work with a CRM tool and databases.
Languages
Fluent written and spoken English is essential;
Fluency in at least one other EU language is a plus (in particular, Italian, Spanish and German).
What we offer
A fixed-term contract under Belgian law;
No probation period;
100%, or part-time negotiable;
40h a week if full-time;
Hybrid (i.e. teleworking in Belgium + presence in the office in Brussels);
Up to six weeks of teleworking from within Europe permitted annually;
A competitive salary, dependent on seniority and skillset;
20 days of legal holidays (prorata) per year + 1 recup day per month (prorata) (= 32 days per year for a full-time position);
A monthly expense allowance, amount depending on seniority;
DKV complementary health insurance, including dental and hospitalisation insurance;
Additional pension plan paid by Finance Watch (6% of gross salary);
A laptop (for professional use);
Full reimbursement of public transportation in Belgium;
Lunch vouchers 8€ per working day (minus personal contribution of 1.09€ per working day);
“Ecocheques” 250€ per year (prorata);
Training possibilities;
A multidisciplinary and multicultural working environment.
Process
